Aspen Discovery for Debian
==========================

Prerequisites
-------------

The PHP 8.4 dependencies come from the deb.sury.org repository, which must
be configured before installing this package:

    apt-get install -y wget gpg lsb-release
    install -d /etc/apt/keyrings
    wget -q -O - https://packages.sury.org/php/apt.gpg | gpg --yes -o /etc/apt/keyrings/sury.gpg --dearmor
    echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/sury.gpg] https://packages.sury.org/php/ $(lsb_release -sc) main" > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/sury.list
    apt-get update

The database can run locally (install the recommended mariadb-server) or on
a remote host; only mariadb-client is required on this server.

Creating a site
---------------

Write a site config ini (see the variables read at the top of
/usr/local/aspen-discovery/install/createSite.php), then:

    aspen-create-site mysite.ini

This creates the site under /usr/local/aspen-discovery/sites/<sitename>,
sets up the database, and enables and starts the site's services.

Service management
------------------

Each site's Solr and background java daemons run as their own systemd
units, grouped per site:

    systemctl status aspen@<sitename>.target      # the whole site
    aspen-svc status <sitename>                   # same, with a unit list
    aspen-svc restart <sitename> solr
    aspen-svc stop <sitename> koha_export
    journalctl -u 'aspen-*@<sitename>*' -f

Which module daemons run is controlled by the Modules page in the admin UI;
aspen-sync-modules aligns the units with the database within five minutes
(immediately once the admin UI hook is in place). The Solr heap can be set
per site with 'solrMemory' in the [Index] section of config.ini (default
3g); other limits go in standard systemd drop-ins, e.g.
/etc/systemd/system/aspen-solr@<sitename>.service.d/override.conf. The
module units also read the optional file
/etc/aspen-discovery/<sitename>/java.env, for passing environment
variables (e.g. JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS) to all of a site's java daemons.

The cron keep-alive scripts (checkSolr.php, checkBackgroundProcesses.php)
are not used on systemd servers: the units supervise the daemons, and the
keep-alive lines are stripped from new sites' crontabs.

Migrating a git based install
-----------------------------

Existing servers installed from a git checkout must be migrated
deliberately; the package refuses to install over /usr/local/aspen-discovery
when a .git directory is present. See aspen-migrate-from-git(8):

    1. Move the old tree aside:
       mv /usr/local/aspen-discovery /usr/local/aspen-discovery.pre-deb
    2. apt-get install aspen-discovery
    3. aspen-migrate-from-git /usr/local/aspen-discovery.pre-deb
    4. Verify the sites, then delete the .pre-deb tree.

/data/aspen-discovery and /var/log/aspen-discovery are untouched by the
migration and by package removal.

Upgrades
--------

    apt-get install aspen-discovery=<version>

The postinst refreshes the per-site Solr config sets, fixes permissions and
restarts the site targets. Run Database Maintenance from the admin UI for
each site afterwards, the same as after a git based upgrade. Do not use the
"complete update" from the admin UI on a package managed server.

Optional pieces from the legacy installer
-----------------------------------------

- mysql_secure_installation and the MariaDB tuning in
  /etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/60-aspen.cnf (installed once, adjust
  innodb_buffer_pool_size to your RAM).
- ClamAV (suggested packages clamav, clamav-daemon).
- The GD rebuild with libraqm for complex text rendering
  (/usr/local/aspen-discovery/install/rebuild_gd_raqm.sh) is optional and
  not run by the package.
